WEBVTT

NOTE Episode: The One Who Flew All Night

1
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:26.163
<v Old Timber>There's that song again. Come close by the pond, little ones. The morning's wide and the willow's full of new green, and I've got one I've been holding for you.</v>

2
00:00:26.513 --> 00:00:48.177
<v Old Timber>Here's what I want you holding while I tell it. When the bravest thing you can do is also the most frightening — when you're shaking so hard you can barely lift your head — does the shaking mean you're not brave? Hold onto that. The answer's in the story. And I'd guess God settled it long before any of us were born.</v>

3
00:00:48.877 --> 00:01:12.369
<v Old Timber>It happened on the kind of spring morning where the whole sky feels scrubbed clean. The creek was bright, the reeds were waking up. And then — high up, so high you'd have missed her — something came down out of the blue. Small. Dark. Fast as a thrown stone, but tired. So tired.</v>

4
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:32.842
<v Old Timber>She didn't so much land as stop flying. Folded down onto the warm bank, wings out, sides going in and out fast. A little swift — the first of her kind ever to set down at this creek. And she had flown all night, little ones, without once touching the ground.</v>

5
00:01:33.542 --> 00:01:46.716
<v Old Timber>Now, the first one to reach her was Bongo the bear cub, who came thundering down the bank with his usual cheerful crash and skidded to a stop a whisker too close. He boomed,</v>

6
00:01:47.066 --> 00:02:00.240
<v Bongo>HELLO new friend! Welcome to the creek! I'm Bongo! Do you want a snack? Do you want a tour? Do you want to meet EVERYBODY at once right now?</v>

7
00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:17.212
<v Old Timber>And the little swift didn't answer. She just pressed herself smaller against the bank and shook. Bongo's big face fell — but Bongo, being Bongo, decided the fix for a scared friend was simply MORE friend.</v>

8
00:02:17.562 --> 00:02:32.408
<v Bongo>It's okay! It's okay! I was scared of the BARN once. The whole barn. For a week. And look at me now, I go in the barn all the TIME. You'll love it here, I promise, do you want the snack yet?</v>

9
00:02:32.758 --> 00:02:40.200
<v Old Timber>Then in zipped Zippy the hummingbird, going so fast she was three places at once, and she squeaked,</v>

10
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:57.866
<v Zippy>A SWIFT! Oh, this is incredible, swifts are the fastest little fliers there ARE — okay, okay, I've already mapped you a flight path of the whole creek, four scenic loops, optimized for wind, we start NOW —</v>

11
00:02:58.216 --> 00:03:22.675
<v Zippy>Loop one is speed. Loop two is scenery. Loop three is speed AGAIN because you'll have warmed up. Loop four is a surprise. The surprise is also speed. Ready? Wait — where'd she go, is she under the reed, why is she under the reed —</v>

12
00:03:23.025 --> 00:03:36.277
<v Old Timber>And from the high branch came Garingo the parrot, all bright feathers and grand flourish, who had been waiting for exactly this moment his whole morning. He spread his wings and announced,</v>

13
00:03:36.627 --> 00:03:55.653
<v Garingo>BEHOLD! A traveler from the FAR and TRACKLESS SKY has chosen — of all the creeks in all the wide world — OUR creek! Stranger, you shall have a WELCOME so magnificent that bards shall SING of it for a HUNDRED years!</v>

14
00:03:56.003 --> 00:04:08.942
<v Old Timber>And up popped the Mudpuppy brothers — Splish, Splash, and Splosh — three frogs and one chorus of helpfulness. Splish leaned in to teach the newcomer the rules,</v>

15
00:04:09.292 --> 00:04:21.996
<v Splish>Rule one of the creek: you must NEVER look directly at the big hill on a Tuesday. Rule two: the reeds are listening. Rule three — Splash, what's rule three —</v>

16
00:04:22.346 --> 00:04:28.728
<v Splash>Rule three is just be nice. ...That's the only real one, actually.</v>

17
00:04:29.078 --> 00:04:43.532
<v Splosh>No no, Splash, you can't put 'be nice' as a RULE, it's far too short, a proper rule needs at LEAST a Tuesday in it. Write down rule four: respect the Tuesday.</v>

18
00:04:43.882 --> 00:04:53.686
<v Bongo>Wait, is it Tuesday? I never know if it's Tuesday. Pip, do YOU know if it's Tuesday — oh no, now I've made it worse, haven't I.</v>

19
00:04:54.036 --> 00:05:10.920
<v Garingo>FRIENDS. PLEASE. You are FRIGHTENING the sky-traveler with your TUESDAYS. THIS is a moment for GRANDEUR, for CEREMONY, for a WELCOME ODE in seven verses, which I have — coincidentally — already begun —</v>

20
00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:19.873
<v Zippy>An ode is seven loops of TALKING, Garingo, that's even slower than scenery!</v>

21
00:05:20.223 --> 00:05:25.247
<v Bongo>Should I do my barn story again? I feel like the barn story is helping.</v>

22
00:05:25.597 --> 00:05:32.685
<v Splosh>Mudpuppies, mudpuppies, EVERYBODY back up, we have clearly overwhelmed her with our excellent rules.</v>

23
00:05:33.035 --> 00:05:38.708
<v Garingo>Agreed! Everyone back up — so they can see the ODE better.</v>

24
00:05:39.058 --> 00:05:43.272
<v Zippy>NObody back up FOR the ode!</v>

25
00:05:43.622 --> 00:05:48.228
<v Splish>Is the ode a Tuesday thing? Should we be writing this down?</v>

26
00:05:48.578 --> 00:06:05.984
<v Pip>Please. Please don't. I'm sorry — I'm so sorry — I don't know any of this. There's RULES? About Tuesdays? And an ODE? I don't even know where I am. I'll go. I'll just — I'll go.</v>

27
00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:16.060
<v Old Timber>And that stopped everybody cold. Because she'd tried to lift off — and her wings just wouldn't. She was too tired to even leave.</v>

28
00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:34.741
<v Old Timber>Now, at the edge of all that stood Fern the fawn, soft-eyed and quiet, watching. And when the little swift tried to leave and couldn't, something moved across Fern's face. Because Fern knew that exact feeling — from the inside.</v>

29
00:06:35.091 --> 00:06:40.428
<v Fern>Everybody. Step back a little. Just — a little.</v>

30
00:06:40.778 --> 00:06:54.344
<v Old Timber>And — would you believe it — they did. Even Garingo lowered his wings. And Fern didn't stand in front of the swift. She sat down beside her, in the grass, the same low.</v>

31
00:06:54.694 --> 00:07:10.428
<v Fern>Hi. You don't have to talk. You don't have to go anywhere. You just landed. That's the whole job right now — you landed, and you're staying still. What's your name?</v>

32
00:07:10.778 --> 00:07:28.915
<v Pip>...Pip. I'm Pip. I — I flew all night and I didn't know where to come down and then there was your creek and I just... fell. I'm not brave. The others where I come from, they aren't scared of anything. I'm scared of everything.</v>

33
00:07:29.265 --> 00:07:37.842
<v Garingo>...Should I do the ode now? ...Is now the ode? Somebody signal me when it's ode.</v>

34
00:07:38.192 --> 00:07:56.094
<v Zippy>Garingo. Garingo. No ode. Read the room. The room is one tired bird and a fawn sitting in grass. I have analyzed the room. There is zero ode in it.</v>

35
00:07:56.444 --> 00:08:02.983
<v Bongo>I'm gonna hold the snack. Just hold it. In case the snack-moment comes.</v>

36
00:08:03.333 --> 00:08:23.221
<v Fern>Pip. Can I tell you something nobody told me, the day I landed here scared out of my fur? You did the bravest thing there is. You showed up. Anybody can do the easy stuff un-scared. Showing up shaking — that's the brave one.</v>

37
00:08:23.921 --> 00:08:43.809
<v Old Timber>Now, here's the part nobody quite caught in the moment — but you will. Fern thought the hard part was over. She was wrong about which part was hard. Because the next morning, Pip was gone from the bank — and Fern found her at the very top of the big hill, looking up at the empty sky like she wanted to leave.</v>

38
00:08:44.509 --> 00:08:54.810
<v Old Timber>So Fern climbed up beside her, and she tried what had worked the day before. She tried welcoming harder. And here's where she got it backwards.</v>

39
00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:09.928
<v Fern>Pip, stay! We'll make it so nice you'll never want to leave. I'll learn every single one of the ways and teach you all of them today, all at once, so you'll fit in by lunchtime, and —</v>

40
00:09:10.278 --> 00:09:20.657
<v Pip>But that's MORE. That's so much more. Everyone keeps giving me MORE, and I can't even fly straight yet, and now I have to fit in by lunch?</v>

41
00:09:21.007 --> 00:09:34.181
<v Fern>Okay. Okay. Then I'll do everything FOR you. You don't have to do anything scary at all, I'll go first on every single thing, forever, and —</v>

42
00:09:34.531 --> 00:09:46.895
<v Pip>Then I'll never DO anything. I'll just be the scared one you carry around. That's worse. I came down on your creek because I wanted to belong somewhere — not be a thing somebody minds.</v>

43
00:09:47.245 --> 00:10:11.129
<v Pip>Back home, when the flock lifts off, they don't even look down. They just go. And I'm always the last one off the ledge, every time, with my heart going like a drum. I thought if I found a new place, I'd land somewhere I wasn't the scared one anymore. But I brought the scared with me. It's right here. It came the whole way.</v>

44
00:10:11.479 --> 00:10:30.740
<v Old Timber>And Fern opened her mouth for fix number three — and stopped. Because she finally heard it. She'd been doing to Pip the exact thing the loud ones did on the bank. Just quieter. And what she'd needed that first scared day was never a bigger welcome at all.</v>

45
00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:50.622
<v Old Timber>And right about then, up the hill came a slow, steady shape — Shelly the tortoise. Young Shelly, who'd been at this creek the day Fern first arrived. She looked at the two of them, and she said one quiet thing, the way she does — to nobody, and to everybody.</v>

46
00:10:50.972 --> 00:11:01.978
<v Shelly>...Funny. The day Fern landed, she didn't need anyone to be braver for her. She just needed one person to sit down next to her and not flinch.</v>

47
00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:10.931
<v Old Timber>Now, this is the part I always slow down for. Because Fern sat with that. And I leaned in and asked her, quiet,</v>

48
00:11:11.281 --> 00:11:21.007
<v Old Timber>Fern... what did you actually need, that first scared morning? Not what you wished you'd had. What did you NEED?</v>

49
00:11:21.357 --> 00:11:35.001
<v Fern>...I needed somebody to believe the brave was already in me. Not hand me more of theirs. Not carry me. Just — believe it was already there. Under all the shaking.</v>

50
00:11:35.351 --> 00:12:02.292
<v Fern>Pip. I'm not going to make you bigger and I'm not going to do it all for you. I think that's how God made every scared small thing — the brave's already inside, packed in tight. I just have to stop sitting on top of it. So. The pond's that way. You set the pace. I'll be right next to you, and I will not flinch.</v>

51
00:12:02.642 --> 00:12:13.988
<v Pip>...The pond's that way. Okay. Okay. I'm still scared. I'm going anyway. Will you — will you stay right there if I do it slow?</v>

52
00:12:14.338 --> 00:12:19.284
<v Fern>Right here. As slow as you want. Off you go, Pip.</v>

53
00:12:19.634 --> 00:12:36.335
<v Pip>I'm doing it. I'm — oh. Oh, I'm UP. I'm still scared! I'm scared and I'm UP! Fern — are you watching? Don't go anywhere — okay — one little loop. Just one. Here I go.</v>

54
00:12:37.035 --> 00:12:55.094
<v Old Timber>Well. By evening, Pip had flown one slow loop over the pond all on her own — wobbly, scared the whole way, and grinning like she'd crossed an ocean. So I asked the friends what they figured it was, in the end, that turned it around.</v>

55
00:12:55.444 --> 00:13:08.461
<v Bongo>Oh! Oh, I know this one. It wasn't the snack. I really thought it was gonna be the snack. It was Fern sitting DOWN. Getting small as Pip instead of big at her.</v>

56
00:13:08.811 --> 00:13:26.948
<v Zippy>And — okay — I learned that 'optimized for wind' is maybe not the first thing a tired person needs. I am writing that down. Loop one: ask if she's tired. I'm keeping the other three loops though, they're really good.</v>

57
00:13:27.298 --> 00:13:41.864
<v Garingo>And I have learned... that a welcome need not be MAGNIFICENT to be a welcome. ...It can simply be a place beside you on the grass. BEHOLD: the grass. ...It's very nice grass.</v>

58
00:13:42.214 --> 00:13:53.638
<v Pip>And nobody made me un-scared. Fern just... believed the brave was already in there. So I borrowed her believing it, until I could.</v>

59
00:13:53.988 --> 00:13:59.090
<v Shelly>...Same as the day Fern landed. Funny how it comes back around.</v>

60
00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:22.854
<v Old Timber>Now isn't that something. And you know — there's a line in the Bible almost exactly that. A man named Paul once wrote a letter to a young friend named Timothy, who was frightened of the big thing in front of him. It's in the Bible, in the book that carries Timothy's name — the second book of Timothy, chapter one, verse seven. And Paul told him:</v>

61
00:14:23.204 --> 00:14:29.247
<v Old Timber>For God didn't give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control.</v>

62
00:14:29.597 --> 00:14:42.379
<v Fern>That's it. That's the thing I needed somebody to say to me, a long time ago. The fear was never the truth of me either. I just couldn't see past it yet.</v>

63
00:14:42.729 --> 00:15:01.180
<v Old Timber>Hear that? Not a spirit of fear. The shaking was never the truth of Pip — the brave was. God packed it in first; the fear was just sitting on top of it for a while. That's the whole of why I wanted to tell you this one. Rest easy, little ones.</v>

64
00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:21.309
<v Professor Hoot>Children, gather round. Today, a fact known to be completely and certainly true — true beyond the smallest doubt — and tied, you'll be glad to hear, to that very visitor our young friends met in the story. A swift. Listen closely.</v>

65
00:15:21.659 --> 00:15:44.789
<v Professor Hoot>A swift lives almost its entire life in the air. It does not perch the way other birds do. It eats in the air, snapping up tiny insects on the wing. It drinks in the air, skimming a sip from a pond as it passes. It even SLEEPS in the air — rising high at dusk and dozing on the wind in little snatches as it glides.</v>

66
00:15:45.139 --> 00:16:11.636
<v Professor Hoot>A young swift may stay aloft for months at a stretch without ever once touching down. 'On the wing,' we say — which is simply the old way of saying while it is flying. It only comes to ground to nest. So when our little visitor flew all night and was bone-tired by morning? Entirely ordinary for a swift. Quite remarkable for the rest of us. Class dismissed.</v>

67
00:16:12.336 --> 00:16:18.953
<v Splosh>Mudpuppies, mudpuppies! Today on Mudpuppy Minutes, we explain... what COURAGE is!</v>

68
00:16:19.303 --> 00:16:31.929
<v Splish>Easy. Courage is when you are NOT scared. None. Zero scared. A truly brave frog feels nothing at all, like a rock. A brave, brave rock.</v>

69
00:16:32.279 --> 00:16:42.423
<v Splash>Hmm. No, wait. I think — I think courage might be... being scared but doing the thing anyway?</v>

70
00:16:42.773 --> 00:16:54.197
<v Splosh>Oh no — no. Splash. That's too easy, and it has 'scared' in it, and brave things can't have 'scared' in them, everyone knows that.</v>

71
00:16:54.547 --> 00:17:03.150
<v Splish>Right! So courage is actually... a kind of HAT. A brave hat. You put it on and WHOOSH, no more feelings.</v>

72
00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:14.349
<v Splosh>Mudpuppies, we have solved it. Courage means: a brave hat that makes you a rock with no feelings. We're SO smart.</v>

73
00:17:14.699 --> 00:17:32.523
<v Old Timber>Now — just so we've got it straight, little ones: Splash had it, right in the middle there, before they talked themselves out of it. Courage isn't feeling nothing. It's being scared, and showing up anyway. That's the honest truth of it — and a fair sight braver than any hat.</v>

74
00:17:33.223 --> 00:17:37.124
<v Ranger Ray>See you next time, somewhere in Shiloh Creek.</v>
